1
00:00:00,360 --> 00:00:09,740
Scientific
papers
describe
what
software
is
meant
to
do,
but
checking
those
statements
against
the
code
repository
can
take
time
and
important
details
can
be
easy
to
miss.

2
00:00:09,740 --> 00:00:13,360
BioAudit
makes
that
comparison
easier
to
inspect.

3
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:22,040
Start
with
a
paper
as
selectable
text
PDF
or
TXT,
then
add
the
code
as
source
files,
a zip
archive,
or a
public
Git
repository.

4
00:00:22,040 --> 00:00:27,880
BioAudit
extracts
candidate
claims
such
as
numeric
settings
and
recognized
method
statements.

5
00:00:27,880 --> 00:00:34,220
It
scans
supported
source
files
for
values,
parameter
names,
function
calls,
imports,
and
their
locations.

6
00:00:34,220 --> 00:00:36,400
It
never
runs
repository
code.

7
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:39,620
Next,
it
compares
each
claim
with
the
evidence
it
found.

8
00:00:39,620 --> 00:00:43,220
A
related
parameter
name
with
the
same
value
may
be
marked
match.

9
00:00:43,220 --> 00:00:46,660
A
related
parameter
with
a
different
value
may
be
marked
mismatch.

10
00:00:46,660 --> 00:00:50,020
A
recognized
method
call
is
reported
as
call
found.

11
00:00:50,020 --> 00:00:52,640
An
import
without
the
call
is
import
only.

12
00:00:52,640 --> 00:00:57,060
For
claims
like
speed
or
runtime,
static
inspection
is
not
enough.

13
00:00:57,060 --> 00:01:02,600
BioAudit
marks
them
result
needs
run
because
they
require
an
actual
execution
or
benchmark.

14
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:09,480
Every
finding
includes
the
evidence
location,
so a
reviewer
can
inspect
it,
search
and
filter
results,
and
export
the
report.

15
00:01:09,480 --> 00:01:13,800
BioAudit
is an
evidence-finding
aid,
not
an
automated
scientific
judge.

16
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:16,940
A
match
does
not
prove
that
code
executes
correctly.

17
00:01:16,940 --> 00:01:19,720
Missing
evidence
does
not
prove
that
a
method
is
absent.

18
00:01:19,720 --> 00:01:26,940
Performance,
reproducibility,
and
scientific
validity
require
further
review
and,
where
needed,
running
the
code
on
the
right
data.

19
00:01:26,940 --> 00:01:33,160
BioAudit,
a
clearer
trail
for
manuscript
claim
to
repository
evidence
with
the
limits
made
visible.
